WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.768
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:06.068 --> 00:00:09.315
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:19.570
Unit forty: Migration and Belonging (Advanced). In this unit, you will adapt your style to different audiences when discussing migration.

4
00:00:20.583 --> 00:00:21.956
transnationalism.

5
00:00:22.656 --> 00:00:28.011
transnationalism means living with strong connections across two or more countries.

6
00:00:28.311 --> 00:00:33.317
Video calls and remittances make transnationalism part of daily life.

7
00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:37.343
Your turn. Say: transnationalism.

8
00:00:40.043 --> 00:00:41.439
transnationalism.

9
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.055
circular mobility.

10
00:00:44.755 --> 00:00:50.063
circular mobility means repeatedly moving between home and abroad for work.

11
00:00:50.363 --> 00:00:55.207
Seasonal farm work creates circular mobility between Cambodia and Thailand.

12
00:00:55.907 --> 00:00:59.500
Your turn. Say: circular mobility.

13
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.712
circular mobility.

14
00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:06.356
citizenship regime.

15
00:01:07.056 --> 00:01:12.272
citizenship regime means the laws and rules deciding who can become a citizen.

16
00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:17.184
Each country's citizenship regime reflects its idea of the nation.

17
00:01:17.884 --> 00:01:21.511
Your turn. Say: citizenship regime.

18
00:01:24.211 --> 00:01:25.874
citizenship regime.

19
00:01:26.883 --> 00:01:28.268
stateless person.

20
00:01:28.968 --> 00:01:34.241
stateless person means someone who is not recognised as a citizen by any country.

21
00:01:34.541 --> 00:01:38.108
A stateless person may be unable to attend school or travel.

22
00:01:38.808 --> 00:01:42.366
Your turn. Say: stateless person.

23
00:01:45.066 --> 00:01:46.346
stateless person.

24
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:48.874
naturalisation.

25
00:01:49.574 --> 00:01:54.894
naturalisation means the legal process by which a foreigner becomes a citizen.

26
00:01:55.194 --> 00:01:58.551
After ten years, she applied for naturalisation.

27
00:01:59.251 --> 00:02:02.182
Your turn. Say: naturalisation.

28
00:02:04.882 --> 00:02:06.151
naturalisation.

29
00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:08.511
host society.

30
00:02:09.211 --> 00:02:13.904
host society means the country or community that receives migrants.

31
00:02:14.204 --> 00:02:18.177
Migrants contribute to the host society in many ways.

32
00:02:18.877 --> 00:02:22.099
Your turn. Say: host society.

33
00:02:24.799 --> 00:02:26.241
host society.

34
00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:28.762
remittance economy.

35
00:02:29.462 --> 00:02:35.061
remittance economy means an economy that relies heavily on money sent home by migrants.

36
00:02:35.361 --> 00:02:38.475
Many villages depend on the remittance economy.

37
00:02:39.175 --> 00:02:42.907
Your turn. Say: remittance economy.

38
00:02:45.607 --> 00:02:47.130
remittance economy.

39
00:02:48.117 --> 00:02:49.524
return migration.

40
00:02:50.224 --> 00:02:54.534
return migration means migrants coming back to live in their home country.

41
00:02:54.834 --> 00:02:58.076
Return migration brings new skills and ideas home.

42
00:02:58.776 --> 00:03:01.951
Your turn. Say: return migration.

43
00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:05.988
return migration.

44
00:03:06.983 --> 00:03:09.262
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:09.562 --> 00:03:13.909
Rithy, imagine telling a minister about migrant workers. How would you sound?

46
00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:21.480
I'd say: 'Circular mobility constitutes a significant component of the national economy.'

47
00:03:22.783 --> 00:03:24.829
And to your cousin who works in Korea?

48
00:03:26.117 --> 00:03:30.209
'Hey, the money you send home really keeps the family going!'

49
00:03:31.517 --> 00:03:35.562
Same idea, very different style. What about a newspaper reader?

50
00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:41.081
'Remittances from workers abroad support millions of rural households.'

51
00:03:42.383 --> 00:03:45.233
Excellent. And would you mention statelessness?

52
00:03:46.517 --> 00:03:50.943
To the minister, certainly: no person should be left without a citizenship.

53
00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:56.908
Grammar focus: Style shifting and audience (extended).

54
00:03:57.208 --> 00:04:00.322
Skilled speakers shift style for each audience.

55
00:04:00.622 --> 00:04:10.217
To a minister: circular mobility constitutes a significant component of the economy. To a cousin: the money you send really keeps the family going.

56
00:04:10.517 --> 00:04:14.653
To the public: remittances support millions of rural households.

57
00:04:14.953 --> 00:04:24.093
C2 speakers choose vocabulary, grammar and tone to fit the listener: formal for officials, warm for family, clear for the public.

58
00:04:25.083 --> 00:04:28.363
Now read and listen. Living Between Two Worlds.

59
00:04:28.663 --> 00:04:33.750
Migration today is rarely a single journey from one country to another.

60
00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:41.926
Many migrants practise transnationalism, maintaining homes, families and friendships across borders.

61
00:04:43.217 --> 00:04:50.882
Circular mobility is common in Southeast Asia, where workers move back and forth for seasonal or contract jobs.

62
00:04:52.183 --> 00:04:59.407
Cambodia's remittance economy supports many rural households, paying for school fees, health care and new houses.

63
00:05:00.717 --> 00:05:07.465
Belonging, however, depends partly on each country's citizenship regime and the rules of naturalisation.

64
00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:16.972
A stateless person, recognised by no country, may struggle to access education, healthcare or legal protection.

65
00:05:18.283 --> 00:05:24.579
A welcoming host society benefits from migrants' skills, while return migration brings new ideas home.

66
00:05:25.883 --> 00:05:33.049
To discuss these realities well, we must adapt our language to ministers, journalists and families alike.

67
00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.944
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:37.244 --> 00:05:42.448
The legal process of becoming a citizen of a new country is blank.

69
00:05:42.748 --> 00:05:48.491
A, naturalisation. B, return migration. C, transnationalism.

70
00:05:52.483 --> 00:05:55.098
The answer is A: naturalisation.

71
00:05:56.583 --> 00:05:58.653
Which sentence is most formal?

72
00:05:58.953 --> 00:06:08.190
A, Remittances constitute a significant share of household income.. B, The money helps a lot.. C, Cash from abroad is awesome..

73
00:06:12.183 --> 00:06:17.329
The answer is A: Remittances constitute a significant share of household income..

74
00:06:18.817 --> 00:06:22.244
Which style suits a message to a close relative?

75
00:06:22.544 --> 00:06:29.216
A, informal and warm. B, official and technical. C, academic and impersonal.

76
00:06:33.217 --> 00:06:36.180
The answer is A: informal and warm.

77
00:06:37.683 --> 00:06:41.982
Someone not recognised as a citizen by any country is a blank.

78
00:06:42.282 --> 00:06:48.362
A, stateless person. B, host society. C, citizenship regime.

79
00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:54.860
The answer is A: stateless person.

80
00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:59.754
According to the text, what do remittances pay for?

81
00:07:00.054 --> 00:07:07.922
A, school fees, health care and new houses. B, only luxury goods. C, government salaries.

82
00:07:11.917 --> 00:07:16.424
The answer is A: school fees, health care and new houses.

83
00:07:17.917 --> 00:07:23.982
Now it's your turn. Part one: How does migration change Cambodian families and villages?

84
00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:34.985
Part two: Answer twice: first formally for a report, then informally for a friend.

85
00:07:41.285 --> 00:07:55.478
Formally: remittances constitute a vital source of income and have improved rural living standards. Informally: honestly, without the money from relatives abroad, lots of families in my village would really struggle.

86
00:07:55.778 --> 00:07:58.010
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:59.017 --> 00:08:04.583
Great work today! You learned eight new words and style shifting and audience (extended).

88
00:08:04.883 --> 00:08:09.149
Today's value is togetherness. Spend happy time with your family.

89
00:08:09.449 --> 00:08:12.102
See you in Unit forty-one. Goodbye!

90
00:08:12.402 --> 00:08:14.857
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

